# Layout

NixOS owns the host (k3s, NVIDIA, CDI). This repo owns what runs on the cluster. Flux bootstraps from `clusters/helios`.

Put a manifest in the directory that matches its kind.

| Path | Put here | Leave out |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `clusters/helios` | Flux `Kustomization` (path, dependsOn, decryption) | Workloads, Namespaces, Secrets |
| `namespaces` | `Namespace` | Anything inside the namespace |
| `sources` | `HelmRepository`, `OCIRepository` | HelmReleases |
| `platform` | Node plugins and cluster DNS | User-facing apps, Certificates, Secrets |
| `platform/cert-manager` | cert-manager HelmRelease | Its Namespace, OCIRepository, Certificates |
| `apps` | Workload HelmReleases | Namespaces, sources, ConfigMaps, Issuers |
| `certs` | `ClusterIssuer`, `Certificate` | cert-manager, Ingress |
| `secrets` | SOPS `*.enc.yaml` | Plaintext, issuers |
| `charts/` | Local chart source | The HelmRelease that installs it |

Wire a new directory with a Flux Kustomization under `clusters/helios`. Leave `clusters/helios/flux-system/` untouched. Update `README.md` and `Taskfile.yml` in the same change (see the docs rule).

A release that needs a namespace, chart source, TLS secret, or SOPS secret waits via `dependsOn` on its Flux layer. Example: open-webui mounts `luisbeu-de-tls` and paperless reads `Secret` `paperless`, so the `apps` layer depends on `certs` and `secrets`. Do not copy those objects into `apps/`.
